    },
    {
      "id": "Ret7lhLS3C9oGGQU9MtIu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 307.6114816855471,
      "y": 976.70808,
      "width": 774.9194946289062,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 352909691,
      "version": 1686,
      "versionNonce": 1957633179,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793022629971,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Warp = 32 threads with consecutive thread indices, cut from one block.\nNever crosses blocks.\n\nThe SM schedules warps, not single threads: a warp is the smallest\nthing it runs.\n\n32 is a hardware choice (every NVIDIA GPU so far); it could\nchange in a future generation.\n\nYour 16x16 blocks: thread index is flattened x-first, so rows 0-1 = warp 0,\nrows 2-3 = warp 1, etc.\n\nWarp is a unit of thread scheduling in SMs. Once a block is assigned\nto an SM, it is further divided into 32-thread units called warps. \n\nWe can calculate the number of warps that reside in an SM\nfor a given block size and a given number of blocks assigned\nto each SM.\n\nFor a block whose size is not a multiple of 32,\nthe last warp will be padded with inactive threads to fill up\nthe 32 thread positions. For example, if a block has 48 threads,\nit will be partitioned into 2 warps, and the second warp\nwill be padded with 16 inactive threads.\n\nIf a block is organized into a 1D array, only the threadIdx.x is used for warps.\nIf a block consists of multiple dimensions of threads, the dimensions\nwill be projected into a linearized row-major layout before partitioning into\nwarps. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Warp = 32 threads with consecutive thread indices, cut from one block.\nNever crosses blocks.\n\nThe SM schedules warps, not single threads: a warp is the smallest\nthing it runs.\n\n32 is a hardware choice (every NVIDIA GPU so far); it could\nchange in a future generation.\n\nYour 16x16 blocks: thread index is flattened x-first, so rows 0-1 = warp 0,\nrows 2-3 = warp 1, etc.\n\nWarp is a unit of thread scheduling in SMs. Once a block is assigned\nto an SM, it is further divided into 32-thread units called warps. \n\nWe can calculate the number of warps that reside in an SM\nfor a given block size and a given number of blocks assigned\nto each SM.\n\nFor a block whose size is not a multiple of 32,\nthe last warp will be padded with inactive threads to fill up\nthe 32 thread positions. For example, if a block has 48 threads,\nit will be partitioned into 2 warps, and the second warp\nwill be padded with 16 inactive threads.\n\nIf a block is organized into a 1D array, only the threadIdx.x is used for warps.\nIf a block consists of multiple dimensions of threads, the dimensions\nwill be projected into a linearized row-major layout before partitioning into\nwarps. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
